FUNDAMENTALS
Scripts evolved from Roman capitals → uncial / half-uncial 
→ Carolingian minuscule → regional Gothic variants → Humanist revival.
Choice of script signaled region, date, purpose, and prestige.

TYPES / VARIABLES

QUADRATA
  Form: angular, compressed blackletter
  Use: formal books, high density, late medieval North

ROTUNDA
  Form: rounded, open Gothic
  Use: Italian / southern manuscripts, more readable curves

ANGLICANA
  Form: cursive, looped English bookhand
  Use: 13th–15th c. England, faster writing

PRECISSA
  Form: sharp, precise, truncated minims
  Use: luxury liturgical / legal texts

INSULAR
  Form: rounded, distinctive early medieval
  Use: British Isles (7th–9th c.), Irish / Anglo-Saxon

HUMANIST
  Form: clear, classical-inspired minuscule
  Use: Renaissance revival of Carolingian / antique models

